call_details.response:
The board has columns 2, 3 and 4 completely filled, so the only legal moves are in columns 0, 1, 5 and 6.

No placement of o produces four-in-a-row:
- column 0 lands at (4,0) and yields at most three o’s on a diagonal;
- column 1 lands at (1,1) and yields at most two consecutive o’s;
- column 5 lands at (1,5) and yields at most two consecutive o’s;
- column 6 lands at (3,6) and yields three consecutive o’s on row 3.

x, however, already occupies the three cells (0,3), (1,4) and (2,5). The unique remaining cell that completes this diagonal is (3,6), which is exactly the square produced by a drop in column 6. Consequently x wins on the next turn unless that square is occupied immediately.

The only move that prevents an instant loss is therefore column 6.

Final Answer: 6

